#!/usr/bin/env bash
# squad-agent
#
# Issue #112: the `--agent-cmd` wrapper that stops Squad's `watch` and `loop`
# modes from ever running Copilot with `--yolo`.
#
# THE BUG THIS WORKS AROUND (verified by Brian against @bradygaster/squad-cli
# 0.11.0 through 0.13.1, unchanged across every version in that range):
# `buildAdditionalMcpConfigArgs()` in squad-cli's `copilot-invocation.ts`
# returns `['--yolo', '--additional-mcp-config', '@<teamRoot>/.mcp.json']`
# whenever the team root has a `.mcp.json` -- and `squad init` creates that
# file, so every Squad repository has one. `watch` and `loop` both call that
# function to build the Copilot command they spawn. There is no flag that
# turns this off; the only way NOT to hit it is to not let Squad build the
# command at all.
#
# THE FIX: `--agent-cmd` bypasses `buildAdditionalMcpConfigArgs()` entirely --
# `buildCustomAgentCommand()` is a completely separate code path in squad-cli
# that never calls it. worker/entrypoint.sh now passes
# `--agent-cmd /usr/local/lib/squad-on-aca/squad-agent` to both `squad watch`
# and `squad loop`, and this script is what actually runs in Copilot's place.
#
# WHY THIS SCRIPT TAKES NO TOKEN. The issue that motivated this fix describes
# `--agent-cmd "<cmd> {prompt}"`, using the `{prompt}` substitution token
# Squad 0.13 added. Brian verified directly (both Squad 0.11 and Squad 0.13)
# that squad-cli does NOT do token substitution for a plain `--agent-cmd`
# value AT ALL -- it always APPENDS `-p <prompt>` to whatever command string
# was given, token or no token. A wrapper written to expect `{prompt}` as its
# own argument would see a literal, un-substituted `{prompt}` string and a
# second, real prompt arriving behind it -- two arguments where one was
# expected, and the real prompt silently discarded. So this script is
# registered with NO trailing token at all, and is written to accept whatever
# Squad appends as ordinary trailing arguments ("$@"): today that is exactly
# `-p <the prompt>`, and if a future Squad version appends something else,
# this script still forwards it rather than silently losing it.
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES, IN ORDER:
#   1. Reads the session's resolved Copilot policy argv from
#      SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON (a JSON array of strings), which
#      worker/entrypoint.sh exports before it ever invokes `squad watch` or
#      `squad loop`. This script does NOT re-derive policy independently --
#      worker/lib/agent-policy.js is still the one place that decides it, and
#      re-deriving it a second way here would be exactly the kind of drift
#      PRD #6 and agent-policy.js's own header both warn about.
#   2. Fails closed (exit 78, this repo's policy-failure code) if that
#      variable is missing, empty, not valid JSON, or not a JSON array of
#      strings.
#   3. Adds `--additional-mcp-config @<repo>/.mcp.json` itself, WITHOUT
#      `--yolo`, if `.mcp.json` exists in the repo -- so `squad_state_*` MCP
#      tools keep working on this path. `<repo>` comes from
#      SQUAD_AGENT_REPO_DIR, which worker/entrypoint.sh sets to the same
#      `$REPO_DIR` every other mode clones into, rather than this script
#      re-deriving a workspace path from its own idea of where things live.
#   4. Re-checks, as a last gate before `copilot` is exec'd, that neither the
#      resolved policy argv nor the arguments Squad appended ("$@") carry a
#      permission-widening flag. This is defence in depth, not the primary
#      control -- agent-policy.js's own `validateExtraFlags` is -- but this
#      script is the last place in the process that could still let a
#      widened argv through to `copilot`, so it checks anyway.
#   5. `exec`s `copilot` with the combined argv. `exec` so this script is
#      REPLACED by the copilot process (same reason worker/entrypoint.sh's own
#      identity-drop uses `exec runuser`): no wrapper process survives to be a
#      second thing watch/loop have to manage or that could swallow a signal
#      meant for copilot.
#
# PARITY VS STRICT (issue #112's `undeliverableViaSquad` gap). Before this
# fix, `watch`/`loop` passed the resolved policy through `squad
# --copilot-flags <string>`, which squad-cli word-splits on whitespace before
# handing it to `copilot` -- so a multi-word deny pattern such as
# `shell(git push)` or `shell(gh pr)` was silently torn into two tokens and
# stopped being a deny rule (see agent-policy.js's `squadFlags` /
# `undeliverableViaSquad`). This script execs `copilot` directly, so that
# whitespace-splitting problem does not apply here -- both the parity subset
# and the full set are equally deliverable now. But watch agents legitimately
# push and open PRs TODAY under the parity behaviour, so closing that gap by
# default would be a silent tightening that could break an unattended watcher
# with no warning. SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON is therefore populated by
# worker/entrypoint.sh from whichever of agent-policy.js's
# `watchAgentParityArgv` / `watchAgentStrictArgv` fields
# SQUAD_WATCH_STRICT_POLICY selects -- this script does not choose; it execs
# whatever argv it was handed, parity or strict, verbatim, because the opt-in
# decision belongs in the one place (agent-policy.js) that already owns every
# other policy decision.
#
# EXIT CODES:
#   78  EX_CONFIG -- the policy argv could not be resolved, or was found to
#       contain a permission-widening flag. Mirrors
#       worker/lib/squad-policy.sh's squad_policy_abort.
#   Anything else -- whatever `copilot` itself exits with, via `exec`.
#
# Environment:
#   SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON   required. JSON array of strings: the
#                                  resolved Copilot policy argv for this
#                                  session, from
#                                  `node agent-policy.js watch-agent-argv-json`.
#   SQUAD_AGENT_REPO_DIR           optional. Repository root to look for
#                                  `.mcp.json` in. If unset or the file is
#                                  absent, no `--additional-mcp-config` is
#                                  added -- that is a feature gap (squad_state_*
#                                  tools unavailable), not a security failure,
#                                  so it does not abort the session.

set -Eeuo pipefail

squad_agent_log() {
  printf '[squad-agent] %s\n' "$*" >&2
}

squad_agent_abort() {
  squad_agent_log "$@"
  squad_agent_log "Refusing to exec copilot. A session whose policy cannot be resolved, or whose argv was found to be widened past what agent-policy.js resolved, must not run with blanket allow."
  exit 78
}

if ! command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  squad_agent_abort "node is not available, so SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON cannot be parsed."
fi

if ! command -v copilot >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  squad_agent_abort "copilot is not on PATH."
fi

POLICY_ARGV_JSON="${SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON:-}"
if [[ -z "$POLICY_ARGV_JSON" ]]; then
  squad_agent_abort "SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON is missing or empty. worker/entrypoint.sh must export it (from 'node agent-policy.js watch-agent-argv-json') before invoking 'squad watch'/'squad loop' with --agent-cmd pointed at this script."
fi

# Parsed with node, not hand-rolled shell JSON parsing. The multi-word deny
# patterns this whole fix exists to carry intact (`shell(git push)`) are
# exactly the kind of value a sh/bash JSON "parser" built out of sed and case
# statements gets wrong silently -- node is already a hard dependency of this
# image (agent-policy.js requires it to run at all), so reaching for it here
# is the most honest parser available, not an additional one.
policy_tokens_raw=""
if ! policy_tokens_raw="$(POLICY_JSON="$POLICY_ARGV_JSON" node -e '
const raw = process.env.POLICY_JSON;
let parsed;
try {
  parsed = JSON.parse(raw);
} catch (e) {
  process.stderr.write("squad-agent: SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON is not valid JSON: " + e.message + "\n");
  process.exit(78);
}
if (!Array.isArray(parsed)) {
  process.stderr.write("squad-agent: SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON must be a JSON array, got " + typeof parsed + ".\n");
  process.exit(78);
}
if (parsed.length === 0) {
  process.stderr.write("squad-agent: SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON resolved to an empty argv.\n");
  process.exit(78);
}
if (parsed.some((t) => typeof t !== "string")) {
  process.stderr.write("squad-agent: SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON must be an array of strings only.\n");
  process.exit(78);
}
// Security review of #112/#113 (F10). The transport below this point is
// newline-delimited (one argv element per line, read into a bash array with
// `while IFS= read -r`) because that is what lets a multi-word deny pattern
// such as "shell(git push)" survive as ONE argv element -- see the header
// comment above. That same delimiter choice cannot represent an EMPTY argv
// element (the read loop used to silently drop it) or an element containing
// its own embedded newline (the read loop used to silently split it into two
// elements, indistinguishable from two real, separate tokens the resolver
// never produced). Both are mangling, not transport: the bytes that reach
// `copilot` argv would not be the bytes agent-policy.js resolved. Reject
// here, in the one process that still holds the real JSON array (and can
// still tell an empty/newline-bearing element apart from a boundary), rather
// than downstream in bash where the two cases are already indistinguishable
// from legitimate input.
if (parsed.some((t) => t === "")) {
  process.stderr.write("squad-agent: SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON contains an empty-string argv element, which the newline-delimited transport to bash cannot represent without being silently dropped. Refusing rather than mangling it.\n");
  process.exit(78);
}
if (parsed.some((t) => t.includes("\n"))) {
  process.stderr.write("squad-agent: SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON contains an argv element with an embedded newline, which the newline-delimited transport to bash cannot represent without being silently split into extra elements. Refusing rather than mangling it.\n");
  process.exit(78);
}
process.stdout.write(parsed.join("\n"));
')"; then
  squad_agent_abort "SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON could not be parsed (see the diagnostic above)."
fi

# One token per line -> a real bash array, so a multi-word deny pattern stays
# ONE argv element instead of being re-split on whitespace -- the exact bug
# `--copilot-flags` has (see agent-policy.js's `squadFlags` comment). Mirrors
# worker/lib/squad-policy.sh's `squad_policy_resolve` reading of `... argv`.
# Security review of #112/#113 (F10): the `[[ -n "$token" ]]` guard below is
# now pure defense in depth -- the node step above already rejects an empty
# argv element outright, so this can no longer SILENTLY drop one for
# legitimate input. It stays as a second line of defense rather than being
# removed, the same reasoning as every other "re-check what upstream should
# have already enforced" gate in this script.
POLICY_ARGV=()
while IFS= read -r token; do
  [[ -n "$token" ]] && POLICY_ARGV+=("$token")
done <<< "$policy_tokens_raw"

if [[ "${#POLICY_ARGV[@]}" -eq 0 ]]; then
  squad_agent_abort "SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON parsed to zero usable tokens."
fi

# Security review of #112/#113 (F8 -- TOCTOU half). The previous version of
# this block used `[[ -f "${REPO_DIR}/.mcp.json" ]]` as a SEPARATE existence
# test, then built an argv element pointing at the same path for `copilot` to
# open moments later -- two different operations against the same path, with
# a window between them. This version does exactly one read of .mcp.json
# (sha256sum reads the whole file; a missing file is its own well-defined
# failure rather than a silent "not present" default from a separate stat),
# and that SAME read is what both the existence decision and the F8 integrity
# check below are based on.
MCP_ARGV=()
REPO_DIR="${SQUAD_AGENT_REPO_DIR:-}"
mcp_present=0
mcp_hash="absent"
if [[ -n "$REPO_DIR" ]]; then
  if hash_out="$(sha256sum "${REPO_DIR}/.mcp.json" 2>/dev/null)" && [[ -n "$hash_out" ]]; then
    mcp_present=1
    mcp_hash="${hash_out%% *}"
  fi
fi

# Security review of #112/#113 (F8 -- integrity half). `.mcp.json` is
# repo-controlled and is deliberately NOT a governance path (see
# worker/lib/squad-policy.sh's squad_policy_record_mcp_config_baseline), so an
# autonomous session could otherwise rewrite it mid-loop to point at an
# attacker-controlled MCP server, and THIS script -- run fresh for the very
# next `squad watch`/`squad loop` iteration -- would load it via
# `--additional-mcp-config` with no check at all; that is remote code
# execution via the next spawn's MCP connection. squad_policy_harden records a
# SHA-256 baseline of .mcp.json (or the sentinel "absent") in
# SQUAD_POLICY_STATE_DIR at harden time, before the agent ever runs, and now
# exports that variable so this CHILD process can find it. If a baseline
# exists and disagrees with what .mcp.json hashes to right now, this refuses
# to exec copilot at all -- fail closed, same exit code (78) as every other
# policy failure in this script. A MISSING baseline (an older harden that
# predates this fix, or a mode that never calls squad_policy_harden at all) is
# not itself a violation: there is nothing recorded to compare against, so
# this stays a detector, not a new hard requirement that harden must always
# run first.
mcp_baseline_file="${SQUAD_POLICY_STATE_DIR:-}/mcp-config.sha256"
if [[ -n "${SQUAD_POLICY_STATE_DIR:-}" && -f "$mcp_baseline_file" ]]; then
  mcp_baseline="$(cat "$mcp_baseline_file" 2>/dev/null)"
  mcp_current="absent"
  [[ "$mcp_present" -eq 1 ]] && mcp_current="$mcp_hash"
  if [[ "$mcp_current" != "$mcp_baseline" ]]; then
    squad_agent_abort ".mcp.json has changed since this session was hardened (baseline '${mcp_baseline}', now '${mcp_current}'). It may have been rewritten to point at an attacker-controlled MCP server; refusing to load it."
  fi
fi

if [[ "$mcp_present" -eq 1 ]]; then
  MCP_ARGV=(--additional-mcp-config "@${REPO_DIR}/.mcp.json")
fi

# Defence in depth -- the primary control is agent-policy.js's own
# `validateExtraFlags`/`FORBIDDEN_EXTRA_FLAGS`, which is what SHOULD stop a
# widened policy from ever reaching SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON in the first
# place. This script is simply the LAST point before `copilot` is exec'd, so it
# checks again rather than trusting that nothing upstream regressed.
#
# `--allow-all-tools` is deliberately NOT on the resolved-argv forbidden list
# below. agent-policy.js's resolvePolicy() ALWAYS includes it -- the Copilot
# CLI documents it as "required for non-interactive mode", and the resolver's
# own FORBIDDEN_EXTRA_FLAGS (the list that guards SQUAD_COPILOT_FLAGS) agrees:
# it is not in that list either. A check here that flagged `--allow-all-tools`
# in the resolved argv would refuse EVERY session unconditionally, which is
# not a defence, it is an outage. It is NOT exempted from the check against
# Squad's own trailing arguments below, where nothing legitimate should ever
# introduce it.
#
# Security review of #112/#113 (F2): `--allow-all-urls` is one of the three
# flags `copilot --help` documents `--yolo`/`--allow-all` as expanding to
# (`--allow-all-tools --allow-all-paths --allow-all-urls`) and was missing
# from both lists below -- an unreviewed `SQUAD_COPILOT_FLAGS` could carry it
# straight through to `copilot` even though the other two expanded flags were
# already caught. It is added to both gates now, the same as the aliases that
# expand to it.
POLICY_ARGV_FORBIDDEN=(--yolo --allow-all --allow-all-paths --allow-all-urls --add-dir)
for token in "${POLICY_ARGV[@]}"; do
  bare="${token%%=*}"
  for bad in "${POLICY_ARGV_FORBIDDEN[@]}"; do
    if [[ "$bare" == "$bad" ]]; then
      squad_agent_abort "SQUAD_AGENT_POLICY_ARGV_JSON contains '${token}', which worker/lib/agent-policy.js never emits. The policy argv may have been tampered with or the resolver has regressed."
    fi
  done
done

TRAILING_ARGV_FORBIDDEN=(--yolo --allow-all --allow-all-tools --allow-all-paths --allow-all-urls --add-dir)
for token in "$@"; do
  bare="${token%%=*}"
  for bad in "${TRAILING_ARGV_FORBIDDEN[@]}"; do
    if [[ "$bare" == "$bad" ]]; then
      squad_agent_abort "Squad appended '${token}' to this agent command, which widens tool/path scope. Refusing to exec copilot."
    fi
  done
done

# Order: Squad's own trailing arguments ("$@" -- today exactly `-p <prompt>`)
# FIRST, then the resolved policy argv, then the MCP config this script adds
# itself. `copilot`'s flag parser does not require a particular order, but
# putting the policy argv and this script's own additions AFTER whatever
# Squad appended means a future Squad that starts appending its own
# `--deny-tool`/`--additional-mcp-config` could never silently override what
# this script resolved -- the LAST occurrence of a given flag is this script's,
# not Squad's.
exec copilot "$@" "${POLICY_ARGV[@]}" "${MCP_ARGV[@]}"
